endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Here, the root node is the subtraction operator. Its left child is the division operation and its right child is p. The division operation has multiplication as its left child and w as its right child. The multiplication has the addition operation as its left child and z as its right child. Finally, x and y are the operands of the addition operation.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Temporary variables are very useful in intermediate code generation. A complex expression is divided into smaller three-address instructions, and every temporary stores one intermediate result. This makes the code easier for the compiler to analyze and translate into machine instructions.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Temporary variables also help optimization. If an intermediate result is required again, the compiler can reuse)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (the stored value instead of calculating it again. They also make register allocation easier because the compiler can determine when a temporary is created and when its value is no longer needed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Another advantage is that the evaluation order becomes clear. In this example, t1 must be calculated before t2, t2 before t3, and t3 before t4. Therefore, the sequence can be easily converted into target-machine)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [11]
>>
